Each operation acts on the live game at the moment it is called. A pose takes
only the parameters its heading names, arranges the running game through the same
systems play uses, and returns nothing; a reading takes no parameters, returns
plain data built at the call, and changes nothing. A pose sets one thing, and the
game's own tick, turning, collision, pellet placement, and scoring run from there
exactly as they do in play, so a scenario driven from code behaves exactly like
one played by hand. Coil runs in one level for the whole session and every screen
is a value of the state's screen field, so a pose that changes the screen takes
effect at the call rather than riding a level transition. `src/game.ts` declares
and exports its type, `CoilDebugApi`.

