{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable. Three
properties make it so.

- A fixed timestep, decoupled from rendering. The simulation advances in whole
  ticks of `TICK_SECONDS` (`0.125`), fed by the elapsed time the game is handed
  and carrying the remainder, as `specs/movement.md` states. An interval of game
  time therefore reaches the same state however it was divided into updates, and
  drawing advances nothing.
- A render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is
  handed, independent of a canvas, of the loop that measured it, and of
  wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from
  the renderer. Where the next pellet lands is decided from the board alone,
  never from anything the renderer holds.
- Seeded randomness. Every draw the game makes, which is the cell each pellet
  spawns on, runs off a generator `reset` seeds, and the game keeps that
  generator's whole state, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces
  the same pellet sequence exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the
game reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and
the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for the overlay.
That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key bindings
`specs/controls.md` fixes, so a dispatched keyboard event steers the snake, works
the menus, pauses and mutes exactly as a player's key does, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and a scripted clock takes it off real time, it drives the
actions the game registers so a dispatched keyboard event steers the snake
exactly as a player's key does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and a scripted clock takes it off real time, it drives the
actions the player controller reads so a dispatched keyboard event steers the
snake exactly as a player's key does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Coil's, under Diagnostics below.

## The driver

The driver is the hold the surface takes on the game's moving parts, and it is
three switches rather than one, because a scenario holds one faculty still while
it watches another. Each switch is on when the game is played, each is restored
by `reset`, each is set by its own operation below, and each is reported by the
snapshot.

| Switch | While it is on | While it is off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `steering` | Step 1 of the tick takes the oldest buffered turn and applies it, and a steering request the player makes is taken into the buffer. | Step 1 takes nothing and no request is taken into the buffer, so `turns` stays empty and `dir` holds the value it carries. The head still advances each tick along `dir`. |
| `travel` | Steps 2 to 5 of the tick run: the head advances one cell, collision resolves, the chain grows or drops its tail, and an eaten pellet scores. | The head does not advance, so steps 2 to 5 do not run: nothing collides, nothing is eaten, and the chain holds the cells it stands on however long the scenario runs. |
| `pelletRespawn` | Step 5 places the next pellet as soon as one is eaten, drawn from the valid set `specs/board.md` defines. | Step 5 places none, so an eaten pellet leaves the board without one. No cell is looked for, so the board-cleared ending is not reached this way. |

Step 6 runs on every tick whatever the switches say, so the combo window drains
by `TICK_SECONDS` and lapses on schedule even while the snake is held still, and
`ticks` counts every tick that resolves.

A switch changes only what it names. Nothing is moved, no figure changes, no cue
plays, and the game carries on drawing the board as it stands. Turning a switch
back on resumes that faculty from wherever the game stands, with no catching up
for the ticks it was off.

The snake is the one body the board always holds, so no operation takes it off
the board. A scenario that is not about the snake poses whatever chain suits it
and turns `travel` off, which leaves the chain occupying its cells and doing
nothing at all.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
The pellet and the obstacle cells are placed and removed outright.
{{else}}
The pellet is placed and removed outright.
{{/if}}

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`COIL_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans, and `col`
and `row` are the integer cell coordinates `specs/board.md` defines.

Each pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands, so a
caller that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the order it
wants them. Nothing a caller does not ask for happens.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<CoilState>`.
A pose returns the next `CoilState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call
fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant. So is a call whose subject is
not in the condition the operation states, such as a menu index no menu on the
current screen holds.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop feeding elapsed time to the simulation, so no tick runs until
`advance` runs one. `setAutoStep(true)` returns the game to advancing itself,
which is how a build starts and how it is played. It changes no game state.

Drawing and input are unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering and keeps
reading the actions, so a menu still answers a key press and a steering request
still lands in the buffer while the simulation is held. The snapshot reports the
switch as `autoStep`.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the tick
resolves exactly as `specs/movement.md` states and the canvas reflects it.

The simulation consumes elapsed time into whole ticks and carries the remainder,
so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` both run eight ticks and reach the same
state.

`seconds` is at least `0` and `frames` is a whole number of at least `1`.
Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a reproducible scenario calls `setAutoStep(false)`
first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every field the snapshot reports to its opening value: the `title`
screen with `menuIndex` at `0`, the score and the best score at `0`, the combo
multiplier at `1` with its window closed, `ticks` and `simTime` at `0`, the snake
back to the starting chain of `specs/board.md` facing `right`, an empty turn
buffer, no live pellet,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
the obstacle course back to `OBSTACLE_CELLS`,
{{else}}
no obstacle cell on the board,
{{/if}}
and all three driver switches on. `options` is optional, and `options.seed` seeds
the pellet generator, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A reset also re-arms manual stepping, leaving the game off the wall clock, so
`autoStep` reads `false` after one rather than the `true` a freshly loaded build
opens on.
{{/if}}

`muted` is untouched, because muting is a player preference rather than a value a
round opens with.

A reset leaves the game indistinguishable from a freshly started session, so
anything else the build keeps across frames is derived from what a reset
restores.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `setScreen(state, screen)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `setScreen(screen)`
{{/if}}

Sets `screen` to one of the six values `specs/ui.md` names, and changes no other
field. The highlight stays where it stood, the board keeps whatever the other
operations posed on it, and the game carries on under its own rules from there,
so ticks resolve on `"playing"` and nothing advances on the rest. Moving to
`"playing"` this way runs the tick over the board as it stands rather than laying
out a fresh round. The screen is the whole record of a round having ended, so a
game moved back to `"playing"` plays on from the board as it stands and never
returns itself to the screen the round ended on.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setMenuIndex(state, index)`
{{else}}
### `setMenuIndex(index)`
{{/if}}

Sets `menuIndex` to `index`, the item the current screen's menu highlights,
counted from `0`. `index` is a whole number the current screen's menu holds. On
`"playing"`, which carries no menu, `0` is the only valid index. It moves nothing
and accepts nothing; a scenario that wants the highlighted item accepted reads
`confirm` in the ordinary way.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScore(state, points)`
{{else}}
### `setScore(points)`
{{/if}}

Sets the running score to `points`, a whole number of at least `0`. Play carries
on from that figure, so the next pellet eaten adds `PELLET_POINTS * M` to it. It
leaves the best score as it stands at the call, and the rule in
`specs/scoring.md` applies from there, so a posed score above the best raises the
best as soon as the game next updates.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setBest(state, points)`
{{else}}
### `setBest(points)`
{{/if}}

Sets the session's best score to `points`, a whole number of at least `0`. It
leaves the running score as it stands, and the rule in `specs/scoring.md` applies
from there, so a best posed below the live score is raised back to it as soon as
the game next updates.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setCombo(state, multiplier)`
{{else}}
### `setCombo(multiplier)`
{{/if}}

Sets the combo multiplier `M` to `multiplier`, a whole number in
`[1, COMBO_MAX]`. It leaves the window as it stands, so posing a live combo is
this call followed by `setComboWindow`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setComboWindow(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setComboWindow(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the simulation time left on the combo window, in seconds, from `0` to
`COMBO_WINDOW` (`3.5`). At `0` the window is closed and the next pellet scores at
an `M` of `1`. It leaves the multiplier as it stands. The window drains from the
posed value at step 6 of each tick, on the ordinary schedule.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSnake(state, cells)`
{{else}}
### `setSnake(cells)`
{{/if}}

Poses the snake as the chain `cells`, a list of `{ col, row }` in the shape the
snapshot reports them, from the head at index `0` to the tail at the last index.
The chain replaces whatever the snake was, so its length becomes the length of
`cells`. A valid chain holds at least one cell, every cell is an interior cell
carrying no obstacle, each cell after the first is orthogonally adjacent to the
one before it, and no cell is repeated.

It sets the chain alone. The direction, the turn buffer, the pellet, and the
score are left as they stand, so a scenario that wants the snake facing a
particular way calls `setDirection` as well. The next tick advances and resolves
collision from exactly this chain.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDirection(state, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setDirection(dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets the direction the snake is travelling in to `dir`, one of `"up"`, `"down"`,
`"left"`, and `"right"`. It leaves the buffered turns as they stand and moves the
snake nowhere. The next tick advances the head in this direction unless step 1
turns it first.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearTurns(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearTurns()`
{{/if}}

Empties the buffer of steering requests, leaving `turns` empty. The direction the
snake travels in is untouched, and nothing is moved: a request taken out this way
was never applied, so it turns nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSnakeSteering(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setSnakeSteering(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns the snake's steering on or off, as the driver above describes. Steering is
on by default and a `reset` restores it. With it off the snake keeps the
direction it holds however long the scenario runs, whatever keys are pressed, and
its travel is untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSnakeTravel(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setSnakeTravel(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns the snake's travel on or off, as the driver above describes. Travel is on
by default and a `reset` restores it. With it off the chain holds the cells it
stands on however long the scenario runs, and its steering is untouched: a
buffered turn is still taken and `dir` still changes, so the snake sets off in
the new direction the moment travel is turned back on.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPellet(state, col, row)`
{{else}}
### `setPellet(col, row)`
{{/if}}

Places the live pellet on cell `(col, row)`, replacing whatever pellet was on the
board. The cell is an interior cell holding no snake segment and no obstacle. The
next tick eats it normally when the head advances into it, and the eat scores,
grows the chain, and places the next pellet exactly as an ordinary eat does.
Placing a pellet is not spawning one, so the generator is not drawn from and the
seeded sequence is left where it stands.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearPellet(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearPellet()`
{{/if}}

Takes the pellet off the board, leaving `pellet` at `null`. Nothing is eaten, so
it scores nothing, grows nothing, and places nothing in its stead. The board
stays without a pellet until one is placed or a real eat replaces it, and the
round carries on.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPelletRespawn(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setPelletRespawn(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns the pellet's respawn on or off, as the driver above describes. Respawn is
on by default and a `reset` restores it. With it off an eaten pellet is not
replaced, so a scenario watching one eat is not met by a pellet landing somewhere
it did not choose.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearObstacles(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearObstacles()`
{{/if}}

Takes every obstacle cell off the board at once, leaving `obstacles` empty. The
cells become ordinary interior cells: the head crosses them safely and a pellet
may spawn on them. The wall border is untouched, because the border is the
board's edge rather than an obstacle. The board stays clear until a call adds an
obstacle back, or until a `reset` lays the course again.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `addObstacle(state, col, row)`
{{else}}
### `addObstacle(col, row)`
{{/if}}

Adds one obstacle cell at `(col, row)`, an interior cell holding no snake segment
and not the pellet's cell. A cell that already carries an obstacle is left as it
is. The added cell is fatal to the head and closed to a pellet spawn from the
call onward, exactly as a cell of the laid course is, and it is drawn the same
way.
{{/if}}

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover" | "cleared",
  menuIndex: <number>,   // the highlighted item, 0 on a screen with no menu
  mode: {{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}"maze"{{else}}"classic"{{/if}},
  score: <number>,
  best: <number>,        // the best score of the session
  combo: <number>,       // the multiplier M, in [1, COMBO_MAX]
  comboWindow: <number>, // seconds left on the window; 0 is closed
  muted: <boolean>,
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,   // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  ticks: <number>,       // ticks resolved since the last reset
  simTime: <number>,     // simulation time accumulated since then, in seconds
  dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",
  turns: ["up" | "down" | "left" | "right"],  // buffered requests, oldest first
  snake: [{ col: <number>, row: <number> }],  // head first, tail last
  pellet: { col: <number>, row: <number> } | null,
  obstacles: [{ col: <number>, row: <number> }],
  steering: <boolean>,       // whether the snake's steering is running
  travel: <boolean>,         // whether the snake's body travels
  pelletRespawn: <boolean>,  // whether an eaten pellet is replaced
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. `snake` lists the
chain from the head at index `0` to the tail at the last index, so its length is
the snake's length in cells. `turns` lists the steering requests waiting on the
buffer, oldest first, and holds at most `TURN_QUEUE_MAX` of them. `pellet` is the
live pellet, and it is `null` only when no pellet is on the board, which is the
case on the `cleared` screen.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
`obstacles` lists the obstacle cells currently on the board, in any order, which
is the course `specs/mode.md` fixes until a call changes it.
{{else}}
`obstacles` is empty, because this mode places no obstacle cell.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
`autoStep` is `true` while the frame loop advances the simulation from the wall
clock, which is how a build starts and how it is played, and `false` while
`setAutoStep(false)` holds the simulation off it.

{{/if}}
Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports
is what the game holds. Four are kept honest rather than posed.

| Field | Kept honest by |
| --- | --- |
| `muted` | Mirroring the runtime's mute bit into the state every frame. |
| `ticks` | Counting every tick the simulation resolves, from `0` at a `reset`. |
| `simTime` | Accumulating the delta time of every update on the `playing` screen, from `0` at a `reset`. |
| `mode` | The one mode the game ships, so it is the same on every screen. |

## Diagnostics

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{else}}
The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{/if}}
Register at least the current `screen`, the score and the best score, the combo
multiplier and the seconds left on its window, the snake's direction, its length,
and its head cell, and the pellet cell, the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep
each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so
watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides it,
it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it. It is
a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep it
visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Coil's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<CoilState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), and keeping
it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Coil's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel, toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), and
keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
